# Flow-census probes whose verdict does not meet the documented promise, and why.
#
# THE UNIVERSE OF THIS FILE IS THE CENSUS in
# packages/tools/src/flow_census_probes.zig: four label families, seven sinks,
# the callee kinds listed there, and the hand-written probes. Each probe has an
# expected verdict (refuse, refuse_or_unprove, hold) that the table in that file
# derives from the documented promise and the owner decisions, never from the
# checker's current behavior.
#
# The gate is packages/tools/src/flow_census_gate.zig, which runs under
# `zig build test-flow-census`. A probe whose observed verdict differs from its
# expected verdict needs one row here:
#
#   PROBE OUTCOME MECHANISM
#
#   PROBE      the probe name, exactly. A pattern is not accepted, so a row can
#              never hide a regression in a probe it was not written for.
#   OUTCOME    what the probe does today: fail_open (the checker proves a
#              property that the probe falsifies), over_refusal (the checker
#              refuses or leaves unproven a probe that leaks nothing), or weak
#              (the checker leaves a leak unproven where it should refuse).
#   MECHANISM  the plan unit that closes the probe. The set is closed:
#
#     F1   a sink is silent while a called function is summarized
#     F1b  an unresolved call whose result is discarded never runs its sinks
#     F1c  recursion stops at the active frame and never widens its labels
#     F2   a captured value carries no label
#     F3   a re-walked callee keeps stale labels
#     F4   HTML built in a callee and discarded is reported as a leak
#     F5   a return-path, concise-body, or nested-expression sink is not checked
#     F6   a credential read through a request passed to a helper has no label
#     F7   a helper imported from another file is walked with unlabelled
#          parameters and its diagnostics are discarded
#     F10  a credential in an egress body is not a sink (owner decision Q6 (a))
#     G    a module-level declaration carries no label
#
#   The plan is docs/plans/2026-10-08-flow-checker-sinks-in-callees.md. A probe
#   that needs several units carries the unit that lands last, because that is
#   the unit that makes the probe match. Every credential row for an egress body
#   therefore carries F10, whichever other unit it also needs.
#
# The gate fails on each of these, and each has its own probe in the gate's
# unit tests:
#
#   unexplained_mismatch  a probe that misses its expected verdict, with no row
#   wrong_outcome         a row whose outcome differs from the observed outcome
#   stale_row             a row for a probe that now matches: delete the row
#   unknown_probe_row     a row for a probe that does not exist
#   duplicate_row         two rows for one probe
#   empty_mechanism       a row with no mechanism
#   unknown_mechanism     a mechanism outside the closed set above
#   unknown_outcome_row   an outcome other than fail_open, over_refusal, weak
#
# The list only ratchets down. A unit that fixes the checker deletes the rows it
# closes in the same commit, and edits the expected-verdict table in
# flow_census_probes.zig where the promise itself changes. A probe that reaches
# no verdict at all (no_verdict) is never allowlisted: fix the probe.
#

